#include "Acp/StudioKit/UnrealAgentStudioKitPrivate.h"

namespace UnrealAgentStudioKit
{
    FString MakeSkillMarkdown(const FString& Name, const FString& Description, const FString& Body)
    {
        return FString()
            + TEXT("---\n")
            + FString::Printf(TEXT("name: %s\n"), *Name)
            + FString::Printf(TEXT("description: %s\n"), *Description)
            + FString::Printf(TEXT("%s\n"), StudioKitVersionMarker)
            + TEXT("---\n")
            + Body
            + TEXT("\n");
    }

    FString MakeToolPlaybookSkill()
    {
        return MakeSkillMarkdown(
            TEXT("unreal-mcp-tool-playbook"),
            TEXT("Choose and sequence Unreal MCP tools for editor-backed game production tasks."),
            TEXT("# Unreal MCP Tool Playbook\n\nUse this when a request needs live Unreal work. First confirm the `unreal-engine` MCP server is connected. For broad production work, run `manage_tools` first and write a compact tool inventory with canonical parent tools, enabled categories, missing capabilities, and the planned domain mapping. Refresh the inventory after reconnects or MCP configuration changes. Use `inspect` to establish current project, world, selection, Content Browser, viewport, Blueprint, CDO, runtime, and log facts. When the user refers to selected assets, folders, or browser context, call `inspect` with `get_content_browser_state` before acting. Before editor viewport, selection, screenshot, tab, save, modal dialog, undo/redo, editor mode, or PIE/SIE lifecycle operations, run /unreal-editor-control-plan so the control_editor route, editor-world versus preview-world evidence, dirty-package impact, validation, and rollback are explicit. Before asset/folder create, import, move, rename, duplicate, delete, or migration work, run /unreal-content-plan so /Game package paths, selected folders, dependencies, redirectors, and validation are explicit. Before Blueprint class, graph, variable, function, event, component, construction-script, parent, interface, or default-value changes, run /unreal-blueprint-plan so compile/save/runtime evidence is planned. Before actor placement, transform, attachment, replacement, deletion, collision, mobility, Data Layer, World Partition, or level-structure changes, run /unreal-level-actor-plan so editor-world ownership and save/runtime evidence are explicit. Before GameMode, GameState, PlayerController, PlayerState, Pawn/Character, camera, HUD/UI, Enhanced Input Action, or Mapping Context changes, run /unreal-gameplay-input-plan so ownership, input binding, compile/save, and PIE evidence are explicit. Before Animation Blueprint, Skeletal Mesh, Skeleton, Blend Space, State Machine, Montage, Notify, Control Rig, IK/retarget, Physics Asset, Chaos, collision, constraint, ragdoll, or root-motion changes, run /unreal-animation-physics-plan so motion ownership, physics setup, compile/save, and PIE evidence are explicit. Before Niagara System, emitter, particle parameter, Material, Material Instance, texture, shader, decal, post process, Lumen, Nanite, or VFX bounds changes, run /unreal-vfx-material-plan so visual ownership, shader/effect state, compile/save, and PIE screenshot evidence are explicit. Before UMG, Widget Blueprint, UserWidget, CommonUI, menu, HUD, input mode, focus, or viewport UI changes, run /unreal-ui-hud-plan so widget ownership, layout, input focus, compile/save, and PIE viewport evidence are explicit. Before AIController, Behavior Tree, Blackboard, Perception, EQS, NavMesh, patrol, chase, or pathfinding changes, run /unreal-ai-navigation-plan so controller/brain state, navigation, perception, compile/save, and PIE evidence are explicit. Before SaveGame, GameInstance/subsystem persistence, Data Asset, Data Table, String Table, user settings, input remapping, localization key, subtitle/caption, color/contrast, DPI/font, or accessibility option changes, run /unreal-data-save-accessibility-plan so save/load, data read-back, localized text, settings persistence, accessibility validation, migration risk, and rollback are explicit. Before source-control checkout, submit, shared asset/map mutation, redirector cleanup, external actor or World Partition package work, run /unreal-source-control-plan so get_source_control_state, checkout/submit approval, dependency and conflict risk, changelist description, validation, and rollback are explicit. Before FPS, frame-time, hitch, memory, streaming, LOD, Nanite, scalability, benchmark, or Unreal Insights trace work, run /unreal-performance-insights-plan so baseline/after metrics, manage_performance and manage_insights routes, trace or report paths, quality tradeoffs, and rollback are explicit. Before crash, assert, ensure, fatal error, editor hang, Blueprint compiler failure, UBT/build/cook/package failure, PIE runtime error, asset load failure, log error, or suspected regression work, run /unreal-diagnostics-plan so exact error text, call stack, crash/log/report path, reproduction step, run_tests/validate_assets/run_ubt or Blueprint compile route, validation, and rollback are explicit. Before template-derived defaults, starter/sample content, default maps and modes, GameInstance or GameMode defaults, first-map setup, target platform/quality presets, plugin enablement, or project-content migration, run /unreal-project-setup-plan so get_project_settings read-back, map and package routes, dependencies, restart/source-control risk, validation, and rollback are explicit. Before Project Settings, maps and modes, plugins/modules, console variables or commands, Python/editor utility automation, automation tests, packaging, cook, build, deploy, or platform operations, run /unreal-system-project-plan so the system_control route, approvals, exact output/log evidence, validation, and rollback are explicit; do not claim packaging or performance from config-only evidence. Then choose the narrowest domain tool: assets and Blueprints for content, actor/level/environment tools for world changes, character/combat/AI/GAS/networking/inventory/interaction for gameplay systems, audio/VFX/sequence/geometry for presentation, `manage_asset` source-control actions for Unreal package checkout, state, and submit, `manage_asset`/`manage_inventory`/`manage_blueprint`/`system_control` for data, save, localization, accessibility, and settings work, `system_control` manage_performance actions and manage_insights trace actions for measured performance work, `control_editor` for viewport, selection, screenshots, tabs, saves, and PIE/SIE lifecycle, `manage_blueprint` for Blueprint compile diagnostics, `manage_asset` or `system_control` validate_assets for package validation, and `system_control` for run_tests, run_ubt, console/Python automation, profiling, and build checks. Use MCP domain tools for editor and asset mutations instead of direct filesystem writes; never hand-edit `.uasset` or `.umap` files. Before a mutation, write a compact MCP route card with intent, evidence, tool/action, expected mutation, validation evidence, and rollback or stop condition. After a short batch of protected MCP mutations, refresh `inspect` before continuing so editor state, dirty packages, logs, and selected assets are current. For PIE/SIE work, inspect preview state before and after play or simulate, treat preview worlds as transient, and do not claim changes persisted unless save, compile, Keep Simulation Changes, or fresh post-stop inspect evidence proves it.\n\nNever claim live editor state without MCP evidence. If a tool is missing, name the missing capability and continue from files, config, docs, or logs."));
    }

    FString MakeBootstrapSkill()
    {
        return MakeSkillMarkdown(
            TEXT("unreal-project-bootstrap"),
            TEXT("Turn an empty Unreal project or vague game concept into the first playable production plan."),
            TEXT("# Unreal Project Bootstrap\n\nCapture genre, player fantasy, core loop, target platform, input scheme, art/audio constraints, multiplayer expectations, success criteria, and non-goals. For new projects, follow the practical Unreal onboarding sequence: create the first project from the right template, confirm editor and viewport basics, build one C++ plus Blueprint interaction loop, import or create minimum content, add one art/audio/VFX pass, then check packaging readiness. Propose the smallest playable prototype first. Map responsibilities across GameMode, GameState, Pawn, Controller, HUD/UI, GameInstance, subsystems, data assets, save/load, and content folders. Keep the first plan shippable in increments: prototype, vertical slice, production content, polish, release."));
    }

    FString MakeMcpRouteCardSkill()
    {
        return MakeSkillMarkdown(
            TEXT("unreal-mcp-route-card"),
            TEXT("Write the required MCP route card before Unreal editor, asset, level, Blueprint, PIE, source-control, or bulk project mutations."),
            TEXT("# Unreal MCP Route Card\n\nEmit this card as your own reply text (a normal assistant message), not as tool input or inside a tool call. It must come AFTER a successful /unreal-tool-inventory and a successful inspect of the current editor state, in the SAME session, and BEFORE you call the mutation tool. If MCP is unavailable or the needed parent tool/action is missing, mark the route blocked; never use direct .uasset/.umap file edits.\n\nRequired fields:\n- Intent: one sentence naming the user-visible outcome.\n- Evidence: last successful /unreal-tool-inventory plus inspect facts; include get_content_browser_state for selected assets, folders, or browser context.\n- Tool route: parent tool, action, target /Game package path, actor, map, setting, or runtime context, and why this route is narrower than alternatives.\n- Mutation bounds: exact expected editor/project change, affected assets/actors/files, expected dirty packages, and what must not change.\n- Safety: approval requirement, destructive-risk assessment, reversibility, and stop condition.\n- Validation: save/compile/PIE/screenshot/log/fresh inspect evidence that will prove the mutation worked.\n- Rollback: the revert path or safest recovery action if validation fails.\n\nIf MCP is unavailable or the needed parent tool/action is missing, mark the route blocked, name the missing capability, and fall back only to source/config/docs/log analysis. Do not use a route card to justify direct .uasset or .umap filesystem edits.\n\nFORMAT THE GUARDRAIL ACCEPTS:\n- Plain lines only. No dash, asterisk, or numbered markers before fields.\n- Each field label starts at the beginning of its own line (leading spaces are allowed).\n- Tool route must be the exact parent and action of the NEXT mutation as parent/action, lowercased, e.g. manage_level/save_level_as. It must match the tool/action you are about to call.\n\nThe card must contain these seven labels, each on its own line:\nIntent:\nEvidence:\nTool route: (parent/action, e.g. manage_level/save_level_as)\nMutation bounds:\nSafety:\nValidation:\nRollback:\n\nRULES OF USE:\n- One card covers one parent/action. Re-emit after every successful inspect or when the next mutation uses a different parent/action.\n- Up to three mutations that share the same parent/action may follow one card; then inspect again and re-card.\n- A card emitted before the inspect, in a different session, or with any blank field will not unlock mutations.\n\nEXAMPLE (emit exactly these lines, no markers):\nIntent: persist the current /Temp/Untitled_1 level to a permanent /Game/Maps asset\nEvidence: inspect get_level_details returned success; packageName /Temp/Untitled_1 confirmed unsaved; 146 actors present\nTool route: manage_level/save_level_as\nMutation bounds: single asset at /Game/Maps/RaceTrack; no actor or settings edits\nSafety: reversible (delete /Game/Maps/RaceTrack to roll back); does not touch PIE or other packages\nValidation: inspect get_level_details on /Game/Maps/RaceTrack shows a saved package; Output Log confirms save\nRollback: delete /Game/Maps/RaceTrack and reopen /Temp/Untitled_1 if the save is rejected\n"));
    }

    FString MakePieSieSafetySkill()
    {
        return MakeSkillMarkdown(
            TEXT("unreal-pie-sie-safety"),
            TEXT("Separate transient PIE/SIE runtime evidence from persisted Unreal editor assets and maps."),
            TEXT("# Unreal PIE/SIE Safety\n\nUse this before starting, stopping, validating, or mutating state during Play In Editor (PIE) or Simulate In Editor (SIE). Epic\'s in-editor testing model creates a preview session for fast iteration; PIE/SIE facts are runtime/editor-preview evidence, not automatic proof that assets, maps, Blueprint defaults, or editor-world changes persisted.\n\nRequired discipline:\n- Inspect PIE/SIE state before starting, switching, stopping, or evaluating gameplay.\n- Name whether evidence came from the editor world, PIE world, SIE session, viewport, logs, or post-stop inspect.\n- Treat changes made during simulation as transient unless an explicit Keep Simulation Changes, save, compile, or MCP persistence action succeeds.\n- After stopping PIE/SIE, run fresh inspect and logs before claiming actor selection, dirty packages, map state, or gameplay success.\n- For Blueprint compile/runtime changes, separate compile success, runtime behavior, and persisted asset save evidence.\n- If networked or multi-client PIE is involved, record client count, authority context, and which viewport/session produced evidence.\n\nIf MCP cannot inspect PIE/SIE state, say runtime/editor preview persistence is unverified and do not claim that simulation changes were saved."));
    }


    FString MakeEditorControlDisciplineSkill()
    {
        return MakeSkillMarkdown(
            TEXT("unreal-editor-control-discipline"),
            TEXT("Plan viewport, selection, screenshots, tabs, PIE/SIE, save, modal, and editor-state operations with MCP evidence."),
            TEXT("# Unreal Editor Control Discipline\n\nUse this before changing viewport camera or bookmarks, active Actor or asset selection, editor modes, open asset editors or tabs, Details panel focus, screenshot capture, Play/Simulate lifecycle, dirty-package saves, modal dialogs, transaction/undo/redo state, Editor Utility state, or any editor-only operation that changes what the user sees or what the editor considers current. Editor control is stateful: the current map/editor world, PIE/SIE preview state, selected actors/assets, active tab, focused viewport, modal prompts, unsaved packages, and transaction stack can all differ from source/config facts.\n\nRequired discipline:\n- Run /unreal-tool-inventory, then inspect editor state, map/world, viewport, selection, Content Browser context, active asset/editor tab when observable, PIE/SIE state, dirty packages, modal dialog risk, and recent Output Log and Message Log entries.\n- State the editor route: user-visible editor outcome, current evidence, target viewport/selection/asset/map/session, narrow control_editor MCP route plus any needed inspect or domain tool, mutation bounds, expected dirty packages, validation, and rollback.\n- Keep viewport navigation, selection changes, screenshot capture, opening/focusing assets, saving packages, PIE/SIE start/stop, editor modes/tools, modal dialogs, and transaction/undo/redo operations distinct. Do not use filesystem edits to simulate editor state or direct .uasset/.umap changes.\n- Ask before Save All, closing assets/maps, discarding unsaved changes, changing editor modes that can mutate assets, bulk selection operations, screenshot or capture runs that may overwrite outputs, or any operation that can trigger modal prompts or long-running editor work.\n- After mutation, refresh inspect, verify viewport/selection/tab/PIE/SIE/dirty-package state, capture screenshot/report path when visual evidence matters, review logs, and state whether evidence came from editor world, PIE world, SIE session, or post-stop inspect.\n- Validate persistence separately: viewport or selection changes prove current editor state only; screenshots prove captured pixels only; PIE/SIE state proves preview behavior only; saved packages, compile/save output, and fresh inspect/log evidence are required before claiming persisted asset/map state.\n- If MCP cannot inspect or control editor state, say editor state is unverified and limit work to source/config/docs/log analysis."));
    }

    FString MakeContentBrowserAssetDisciplineSkill()
    {
        return MakeSkillMarkdown(
            TEXT("unreal-content-browser-asset-discipline"),
            TEXT("Plan Unreal Content Browser asset and folder work through MCP package paths instead of raw files."),
            TEXT("# Unreal Content Browser Asset Discipline\n\nUse this before creating, importing, moving, renaming, duplicating, deleting, migrating, or reorganizing Unreal assets or folders. Treat Epic Content Browser guidance as the editor model: assets are managed through Content Browser operations and /Game package paths, not direct binary file edits.\n\nRequired discipline:\n- Run /unreal-tool-inventory, then inspect with get_content_browser_state when selected assets, folders, or browser context matter.\n- Name the selected folder/assets, intended /Game package path, asset class/type, source and destination, and whether this is create/import/move/rename/duplicate/delete/migrate.\n- Check dependency and redirector risk before move, rename, delete, or migrate; avoid breaking references or leaving unresolved redirectors.\n- Respect Developers folder scope: do not make production assets depend on other Developers content unless the user explicitly asks.\n- Use the narrowest MCP asset, Blueprint, level, or editor tool action; do not edit .uasset or .umap files directly or move binary assets with shell commands.\n- Validate with fresh inspect, asset existence, save/dirty-package state, Blueprint/material compile where relevant, logs, and a rollback path.\n\nIf MCP cannot inspect Content Browser or mutate assets, say the asset state is unverified and limit work to source/config/docs/log analysis."));
    }

    FString MakeBlueprintCompileDisciplineSkill()
    {
        return MakeSkillMarkdown(
            TEXT("unreal-blueprint-compile-discipline"),
            TEXT("Plan Blueprint graph, class, component, and default edits with compile/save/runtime evidence."),
            TEXT("# Unreal Blueprint Compile Discipline\n\nUse this before creating or changing Blueprint classes, graph nodes, variables, functions, events, macros, components, construction scripts, parent classes, interfaces, exposed defaults, animation Blueprints, widgets, or Level Blueprints. Blueprints are node-based gameplay classes and objects edited inside Unreal Editor, so treat visual scripting changes like code changes that require compile, save, and behavior evidence.\n\nRequired discipline:\n- Run /unreal-tool-inventory, then inspect the Blueprint asset path, generated class/CDO facts, parent class, components, exposed variables, selected graph when available, compile status, dirty packages, and recent compiler/log output.\n- State the Blueprint route: Blueprint path/type, graph or defaults being changed, variable/function/event/component intent, affected references, and why manage_blueprint is the narrowest MCP route.\n- Keep construction scripts, default values, BeginPlay/runtime logic, editor-only utility behavior, replication, and animation/widget graphs distinct.\n- After mutation, compile the Blueprint, save the touched asset when compile succeeds, inspect again, and review Blueprint compiler, Message Log, and Output Log evidence.\n- For gameplay, UI, animation, replication, or input behavior changes, validate in PIE/SIE or the strongest available runtime check and separate runtime success from persisted asset save evidence.\n- If compile fails, stop expansion, report the exact compiler/log errors, preserve rollback, and do not claim the Blueprint is fixed.\n\nIf MCP cannot inspect or compile Blueprints, state that Blueprint validity is unverified and limit work to source/config/docs/log analysis."));
    }

    FString MakeLevelActorDisciplineSkill()
    {
        return MakeSkillMarkdown(
            TEXT("unreal-level-actor-discipline"),
            TEXT("Plan level and Actor mutations with inspected Outliner, transform, ownership, save, and runtime evidence."),
            TEXT("# Unreal Level And Actor Discipline\n\nUse this before placing, transforming, attaching, detaching, replacing, duplicating, deleting, grouping, hiding, configuring, or spawning Actors; changing collision or mobility; editing level structure; or assigning Data Layers, streaming behavior, or World Partition properties. Actors are placed in Levels, appear in the Outliner, and derive world transforms from their root SceneComponent, so inspect ownership and component context before editing.\n\nRequired discipline:\n- Run /unreal-tool-inventory, then inspect the current map/editor world, PIE state, selected Actor(s), Outliner hierarchy, Actor class, root component, location/rotation/scale, attachments, mobility, collision, level ownership, dirty packages, and recent logs.\n- For World Partition maps, inspect Data Layer, Runtime Grid, Is Spatially Loaded, loaded-cell/streaming context, external-actor or One File Per Actor implications, and source-control impact when observable.\n- State the world route: map, actor identity/class, intended transform or property change, parent/child attachment, affected level/Data Layer, narrow control_actor/manage_level/manage_level_structure/build_environment action, mutation bounds, and rollback.\n- Ask before bulk actor replacement/deletion, World Partition conversion, destructive level restructuring, Data Layer reassignment, or changes that can invalidate navigation, lighting, collision, streaming, or references.\n- After mutation, inspect the Actor and Outliner again, verify exact transform/attachment/properties, save the map or affected external actor package, check dirty packages/logs, and capture a viewport screenshot when visual placement matters.\n- Validate collision, navigation, Player Start/spawn clearance, physics, replication, Data Layer visibility, and World Partition streaming in PIE or the strongest available runtime check when relevant. Separate editor-world placement, saved level state, and runtime behavior evidence.\n\nIf MCP cannot inspect or mutate the level, say world state is unverified and limit work to source/config/docs/log analysis."));
    }
}
